# Clean-checkout verdicts for check-evidence-commands.py -- 2026-10-04

One sentence: the guard asked the filesystem whether a path a document names is there, so
two checkouts of the same commit gave different verdicts, and the red one was CI.

This is the second half of that guard. `docs/verification/evidence-commands-2026-10-04.log`
covers what the two rules find and why the exclusions are shaped as they are; this one
covers the one question both rules rest on -- what counts as present -- and the fact that it
used to be answered from the wrong place.

== 1. What CI said

Run 37179588831 pushed `11cdd8a2`. The `docs localization` job was red on one step of nine,
and every step before it had passed, so the docs rules upstream were not implicated:

$ gh api repos/chao2hang/chaos-code/actions/jobs/111371888182 --jq '.steps[] | [.number, .name, .status, (.conclusion // "-")] | @tsv'
    1   Set up job  completed   success
    2   Run actions/checkout@v4 completed   success
    3   Install ripgrep completed   success
    4   user guide, reference docs and tutorials    completed   success
    5   guard self-tests    completed   success
    6   TODO status document matches TODO.md    completed   success
    7   Committed docs do not cite scratch paths    completed   success
    8   Docs path references resolve or are recorded    completed   success
    9   Documented commands can actually be run completed   failure

Step 9 runs the fixtures and then the gate over the checkout. The log download refuses while
the run is in flight, so the finding was recovered by running that step locally, and the
guard's own repository case carried it. That reproduction is at `778d4dae`, which is why it
names line 406:

    FAIL: test_the_repository_this_guard_was_written_for_is_clean (__main__.ScanTests)
    AssertionError: 1 != 0 : check-evidence-commands: docs/verification/pipefail-report-gate-2026-10-04.log:406: `apps/chaos-ui/node_modules` names a file that is not in the repository, so this command line reproduces nothing (unrecorded)
    check-evidence-commands: 333 files, 1344 command lines, 1 interpreter mismatch(es), 6 dangling path(s), 6 recorded; 1 problem(s)

That fixture runs the shipped gate over the real checkout, which is what makes it the one
case that can disagree with a developer's machine. The line it names is a transcript line,
and it is accurate about the run it captured:

$ grep -n 'du -sh target .git' docs/verification/pipefail-report-gate-2026-10-04.log
    406:$ du -sh target .git apps/chaos-ui/node_modules
$ git show 11cdd8a2:docs/verification/pipefail-report-gate-2026-10-04.log | grep -n 'du -sh target'
    387:$ du -sh target .git apps/chaos-ui/node_modules

At `11cdd8a2` the same text sits at 387, so that is the number the CI message carried; later
batches appended to that log and pushed it to 406.

== 2. The reason it was green here: the file is in this tree

    $ ls -d apps/chaos-ui/node_modules
    apps/chaos-ui/node_modules
    $ du -sh apps/chaos-ui/node_modules
    143M    apps/chaos-ui/node_modules
    $ git check-ignore -v apps/chaos-ui/node_modules/
    .gitignore:33:apps/chaos-ui/node_modules/   apps/chaos-ui/node_modules/
    $ git ls-files apps/chaos-ui/node_modules | wc -l
    0

An installed dependency tree, 143M of it, ignored on purpose and in no commit. The rule that
anchors a claim already asked git -- a path only counts when its first segment is a tracked
top-level entry, which is what keeps `target/` and an absolute scratch path out of the scan
-- but the follow-up question then asked the filesystem. Half git, half filesystem is
exactly the shape of this failure: on the machine that wrote the transcript the directory
exists, so the claim is honoured; on a runner that checked the commit out into an empty
workspace it does not, so the same bytes are a finding.

The matrix below is two depth-1 clones of one commit, `778d4da`, differing only in whether
an empty `apps/chaos-ui/node_modules/` sits in the tree. Each tree ran the committed guard,
then this change:

    tree=clean  commit=778d4da
      node_modules on disk: no
      tracked by git       : 0 file(s)
      ignored by .gitignore: yes
      --- committed guard (answers existence from the filesystem)
          ... pipefail-report-gate-2026-10-04.log:406: `apps/chaos-ui/node_modules` names a file ...
          check-evidence-commands: 333 files, 1344 command lines, 1 interpreter mismatch(es), 6 dangling path(s), 6 recorded; 1 problem(s)
          exit=1
      --- this change (answers existence from git)
          check-evidence-commands: 333 files, 1344 command lines, 1 interpreter mismatch(es), 5 dangling path(s), 6 recorded; every command line can run and every absence is recorded and live
          exit=0

    tree=built  commit=778d4da
      node_modules on disk: yes
      tracked by git       : 0 file(s)
      ignored by .gitignore: yes
      --- committed guard (answers existence from the filesystem)
          check-evidence-commands: 333 files, 1344 command lines, 1 interpreter mismatch(es), 5 dangling path(s), 6 recorded; every command line can run and every absence is recorded and live
          exit=0
      --- this change (answers existence from git)
          check-evidence-commands: 333 files, 1344 command lines, 1 interpreter mismatch(es), 5 dangling path(s), 6 recorded; every command line can run and every absence is recorded and live
          exit=0

One commit, two verdicts before; one verdict after. The real working tree is the `built`
case with 143M instead of an empty directory.

== 3. Why the ignore rule asks git twice

Generated content is what `.gitignore` describes, so a command line reading it is a recipe
with a build step in front of it, not a reference to a file that went missing. Asking git
about the path as written would have reproduced the same split one level down, because the
pattern is `apps/chaos-ui/node_modules/` and a pattern with a trailing slash only matches
directories, which git can only tell from a directory that exists:

    git check-ignore -v apps/chaos-ui/node_modules
        -> exit 1  no match
    git check-ignore -v apps/chaos-ui/node_modules/
        -> exit 0  .gitignore:33:apps/chaos-ui/node_modules/   apps/chaos-ui/node_modules/
    git check-ignore -v apps/chaos-ui/node_modules/react/index.js
        -> exit 0  .gitignore:33:apps/chaos-ui/node_modules/   apps/chaos-ui/node_modules/react/index.js

Both the second and the third question are answerable without a workspace, so `is_ignored`
asks the bare path and the path with a trailing slash in one `git check-ignore -q --stdin`
call and treats either match as generated. The result is cached per path, and only paths
that are neither tracked nor a tracked directory get asked, which on this repository is
seven paths per run.

== 4. The rule as shipped

`Repo.presents(rel)` is now the only way rule 2 answers presence, and it says yes when git
tracks the path, when git tracks something under it (`git ls-files` lists files and never
the directories holding them, so `ls docs/verification` is a claim that holds), or when
`.gitignore` declares it generated. Everything else is a finding, including a file that this
particular checkout happens to have and no commit carries.

The ledger stayed at six rows and the gate did not need a new exemption, which is the point:
a rule that fires on generated output is a wrong rule and gets fixed in the script, and the
stale check would have failed the moment a row stopped describing a live finding.

== 5. Fixtures

Three cases, on top of the nineteen already there:

- `test_generated_output_is_no_claim_built_or_unbuilt` names an ignored directory and an
  ignored file, runs the gate with neither on disk, runs it again with both present, and
  asserts the two stdout bodies are byte-identical. This is the CI-versus-machine contract
  written as a test, and it is the case that would have caught the red job;
- `test_only_this_checkout_having_a_file_is_not_a_clean_verdict` is its mirror: a path
  neither tracked nor ignored, sitting on disk, is still a finding. Answering from the
  filesystem forgave exactly this, so a fix that only special-cased the first case would
  pass one and fail the other;
- `test_a_tracked_directory_can_be_what_a_command_reads` reads two tracked directories and
  one missing one, and counts the findings rather than grepping the message, because a
  finding line quotes the citing document and therefore contains `docs/verification`.

The fixture repository gained `scripts/vendor/` (a directory pattern) and `docs/secret.env`
(a file pattern) under tracked top-level entries, so the top-level filter cannot excuse them
and the ignore rule has to.

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-evidence-commands.py 2>&1 | tail -3
        Ran 22 tests in 1.512s

    OK

