The first run is the CI failure reproduced inside the image, which is the useful direction:
a clean tree is enough, no runner needed. The second and third are the point of the change,
one verdict whether or not the dependency is installed. The last counts 334 files and 1352
command lines because this log is staged in this index and absent from the clones, since
`Repo.scanned()` walks tracked content only. The pre-fix gate on a tree that has the
dependency was not run in the container, because §2 already measured that pair on the host
and the mount makes the two cases the same tree.

== 8. What this does not cover

- A document may still name a path that exists only inside a container (`/src/...`) or only
  after a build whose output is not ignored; neither is a claim about the commit and neither
  is checked. `check-doc-path-refs.py` covers `path-refs`, not command lines, and has the
  same tracked-anchor start.
- The repository case in the fixtures still asserts a property of the working tree, so an
  uncommitted edit to a document moves it. That is deliberate -- it is the case that keeps
  the gate honest about the tree it was written for -- but it is not a check of the commit.
- Ignore rules are consulted as git sees them from the repository root, so a path ignored by
  a user's global gitignore or by `.git/info/exclude` counts as generated here too. That is
  the same direction as the fix and is noted rather than argued away; `.gitignore` is the
  durable half of that set, and it is what the repository's own generated paths are named
  in.
- Rule 1 (wrong interpreter) reads only the script operand and is untouched by this change.

== 9. Reproduce

    git clone --no-hardlinks --depth 1 . /var/tmp/clean-checkout-evcmd
    cd /var/tmp/clean-checkout-evcmd
    python3 scripts/ci/check-evidence-commands.py      # exit 0 with this change
    git show HEAD~1:scripts/ci/check-evidence-commands.py > scripts/ci/check-evidence-commands.py
    python3 scripts/ci/check-evidence-commands.py      # exit 1: the finding CI saw

The two verdicts differ only because that tree has no `apps/chaos-ui/node_modules`, which is
how a runner looks. Re-running the reverted guard after `mkdir -p
apps/chaos-ui/node_modules` turns it green again without one byte of the commit changing,
and that is the whole defect.

    cd /home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code
    python3 scripts/ci/test-check-evidence-commands.py
    git check-ignore -v apps/chaos-ui/node_modules     # exit 1: no match, asked as a file
    git check-ignore -v apps/chaos-ui/node_modules/    # the trailing slash is the difference
